1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,060
I build software on my own, no other developer, no tester.

2
00:00:05,620 --> 00:00:11,360
AI writes most of my code now, and the only thing telling me it's done is the AI that just wrote it.

3
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:19,540
Three jobs for one AI, a password page, a payment page, and one that imports a spreadsheet.

4
00:00:20,160 --> 00:00:26,440
Where it ends up, the second AI finds bugs but can't touch the code, a third that fixes them.

5
00:00:28,340 --> 00:00:31,780
A real ticket, the way I would write one.

6
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:35,820
People forget their password, we reset it by hand for a week.

7
00:00:36,320 --> 00:00:40,080
Three steps, not a word about what could go wrong. That's normal.

8
00:00:43,240 --> 00:00:46,940
Before any of that, I wrote 18 rules the code has to obey.

9
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:50,920
Links expire, two payments at once, only one goes through.

10
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:53,520
I call the last one the double charge.

11
00:00:54,520 --> 00:00:57,420
I never showed them to it.

12
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:02,320
Hand an AI the marking scheme, and it does, and it just does the marking scheme.

13
00:01:02,780 --> 00:01:09,500
And that's not me being unfair, somebody says build me a login page, and every rule stays in their head.

14
00:01:14,300 --> 00:01:17,520
One AI, one go, no review.

15
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:23,480
What most people are doing right now, three jobs under 30 seconds, about $0.08 each.

16
00:01:23,900 --> 00:01:25,700
It did everything the ticket asked.

17
00:01:26,380 --> 00:01:29,420
Eleven of the 18 rules pass, seven are broken.

18
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:37,460
All three you just read, including the double charge, two payments land together, both go through.

19
00:01:37,960 --> 00:01:39,740
And four more underneath.

20
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:50,460
So I added a second AI, I call it the checker, and its only job is to attack that code.

21
00:01:50,780 --> 00:01:54,160
It can't touch anything, all it can do is write a list.

22
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:02,120
The list goes to a third AI, that's the fixer, and that one changes the code.

23
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:06,800
Finding the problem and fixing it are two jobs, so there are two AIs.

24
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:13,780
What comes back isn't a tick, it's this.

25
00:02:14,500 --> 00:02:21,140
Four problems raised, five still open after the repair, it went up, knocked down, and it says so.

26
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:25,300
And the top line, this is a recommendation, not an approval.

27
00:02:25,740 --> 00:02:27,780
It doesn't decide, I do.

28
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:38,340
Same three jobs both ways, same model writing the code,

29
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:42,000
is script scores both, and it can't see which version it's grading.

30
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:52,320
One AI passes 11, three AIs pass 14, four runs of the normal one, three of mine, 11 every time on the left.

31
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:53,960
That's the change.

32
00:02:53,960 --> 00:02:58,320
A reviewer isn't allowed to edit a repair step and a second look.

33
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:02,260
This is the bit I nearly missed.

34
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:06,860
14 three times, but it isn't the same 14.

35
00:03:07,400 --> 00:03:10,820
Two runs, fix four, and break one.

36
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:14,480
The third, fixes three, breaks nothing.

37
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:18,740
So I ran the same pipeline three more times, 12 and 15.

38
00:03:18,740 --> 00:03:24,620
It doesn't reliably fix four, it reliably fixes two, and the total was luck.

39
00:03:27,220 --> 00:03:32,060
The only reason I found that is I'd written every rundown as I went.

40
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:34,500
Every experiment I ran is in there.

41
00:03:34,660 --> 00:03:38,420
The last one is me pulling a step out to see if I was doing anything.

42
00:03:38,820 --> 00:03:39,540
It wasn't.

43
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:48,360
Sometimes the checker is certain that a problem that isn't there,

44
00:03:48,360 --> 00:03:51,080
and the fixer believes it, so prove it first.

45
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:54,680
Four becomes five, six and eight.

46
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:58,700
Same setup, three runs, it stopped being a number I could trust.

47
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:02,980
It blocked two findings that were right, and it hadn't stopped the wrong one either.

48
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:04,880
One and three both ways.

49
00:04:08,740 --> 00:04:09,980
Here's what I got wrong.

50
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:12,960
The gate was grading how well the checker argued.

51
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:16,140
That's the one thing AI is brilliant at faking,

52
00:04:16,140 --> 00:04:20,000
and the finding that hurt me was wrong in a completely different way.

53
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:23,800
Acting on it broke this rule, and breaking this rule is a double charge.

54
00:04:26,120 --> 00:04:30,120
So don't create the argument after every repair, look at the code again.

55
00:04:30,780 --> 00:04:35,160
That version passes 14 up from 11, run the scoring six times,

56
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:37,160
and it lands between 12 and 15.

57
00:04:37,700 --> 00:04:39,280
Every one of them is above 11.

58
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:44,260
It breaks one, it still breaks one and two runs out of three.

59
00:04:44,260 --> 00:04:47,500
Three jobs isn't many, that's the first thing I change.
